  "aaeed8d0-7f9b-4739-ace8-51e75ef1a60c": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p18-rule-block",
     "replacement": "If two successive events $A$ and $B$ in a multistage experiment are **independent**, then:\n\n$$P(A\\text{ and }B)=P(A)\\times P(B)$$\n\nWe can write $P(A\\text{ and }B)$ as $P(AB)$, $P(A,B)$ or $P(A\\cap B)$.\n\nThe order of the factors does not matter: $P(A\\cap B)=P(B\\cap A)$. However, 'A at stage 1, then B at stage 2' and 'B at stage 1, then A at stage 2' are different outcomes, so they can have different probabilities.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p18-rule-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "266c4ae82e2ad6b4dac68c7bf9b85e1a1065080c56fabeaefee185bf2d8095a9",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
